    "question": {
      ":question/id": "83a0e6e5-b8d6-5f44-8055-e8cefad1c538",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}y = 3, x > 0$.\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=3.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})\\,dx & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ y & =x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
