    "question": {
      ":question/id": "910afdeb-5a23-57a2-89b5-dcca6e4cba1d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = 3x$.\nSelect the correct options below.\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First divide the equation by $2$ to write it in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2}y=\\frac{3x}{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{3x}{2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1/2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{x/2}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{2} \\\\ {e}^{x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\cdot y & =\\frac{3x}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x/2}) & =\\frac{3x}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x/2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3x}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts.\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int {u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u=x,{u}^{′}=1,{v}^{′}={e}^{x/2},$ and $v=2{e}^{x/2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x/2} & =\\int \\frac{3x}{2}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{x/2} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\left(x(2{e}^{x/2})-\\int 2{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\right) \\\\ y{e}^{x/2} & =3x{e}^{x/2}-3\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{x/2} & =3x{e}^{x/2}-6{e}^{x/2}+C \\\\ y{e}^{x/2} & =3(x-2){e}^{x/2}+C \\\\ y & =3(x - 2) + Ce^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
