      ":question/id": "e71b7e42-a600-59e3-93ef-a2a4b1fbd207",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = x^{2}e^{2x}$.\nSelect the correct options below.\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {x}^{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot y & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-2x}) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-2x} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{2x}}{3}+C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
